    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle \\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}=\\frac{(n-1)!\\,[k+(n-k)]}{k!(n-k)!}=\\binom{n}{k}$.\n\nUse the factorial definition and a common denominator.",
      "worked": "Starting with the right-hand side,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}&=\\frac{(n-1)!}{(k-1)!(n-k)!}+\\frac{(n-1)!}{k!(n-k-1)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}&=\\frac{k(n-1)!+(n-k)(n-1)!}{k!(n-k)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}&=\\frac{(n-1)!\\,[k+(n-k)]}{k!(n-k)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}&=\\frac{n(n-1)!}{k!(n-k)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}&=\\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}&=\\binom{n}{k}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
